STEPS for Mini-Hackathon
1. View the document with activity descriptions.
2. Select 1 activity for your group to focus on (There are 3 activity options);
3. Plan out your workflow, steps and analysis process;
4. Create a free account for the alphafold webserver using the link here: https://alphafold.com/
5. Begin to work on your tasks
6. Feel free to utilize any online tool/resource that would be helpful in completing your tasks; a list of some options is provided for you on page 2 of the activity handout; also linked here: https://drive.google.com/file/d/16-pc5Q7aTQbNqcsIaWpFoA7-9DXQmTBo/view?usp=drive_link
7. Once your activity is complete, prepare a short slide presentation (~10 slides) that summarizes the results from what your group has found given the activity instructions and your steps taken to solve the problem. This will be shared with the class!

***Tips***
--AlphaFold 3 Confidence Metrics Viewer or the PAE Viewer will allow you to perform some post-modeling analysis along with assessment of all the other metrics provided by the AlphaFold tool.
--Find the best model; Look inside the summary files in order to figure out which of the models has the highest score. 
--Assess your (Predicted Aligned Error-PAE): This will appear as a massive grid (matrix) mapping how much error AlphaFold expects between any position $A$ and position $B$ in your protein.
--If a region has a low PAE score (looks like a dark, solid block on a PAE plot), you can confidently trust the relative orientation of those domains. 
If it's a bright, high-error zone, AlphaFold is telling you, "I know what these domains look like individually, but I have no idea how they sit next to each other."
--The "Predicted TM-score" denotes a score from 0 to 1 measuring the global structural accuracy. Anything above 0.5 means the overall fold is likely correct.
--"Interface pTM" score: If your sequence has multiple chains or interacting molecules, this scores how confident AlphaFold is in the interaction interface (0.6 to 0.8 is a gray area); >0.8 means highly confident.
